<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ⚠️ THE ENCODING DECLARATION IS TRUE, AND THAT MATTERS HERE. This file is stored
     as UTF-8 and says so. Task Scheduler EXPORTS its XML as UTF-16, so most
     examples on the web carry an encoding="UTF-16" header; copying that header
     onto UTF-8 bytes produces a file that `schtasks /Create /XML` rejects with an
     unhelpful parse error. Register it with PowerShell's Register-ScheduledTask
     (README), which takes the XML as a string and does not care.
-->

<!--
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Windows TASK SCHEDULER definition for the ctxroute HTTP injection gate.
  NOT INSTALLED. Edit the marked values, then register it as the USER
  (no elevation), see service/README.md for the exact command.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  🛑 WHY THIS IS A SCHEDULED TASK AND NOT A WINDOWS SERVICE — settled by the
     official API contract, do not reopen it. A Windows Service is not "a process
     the SCM starts": it is a process that must SPEAK to the SCM.
     StartServiceCtrlDispatcher (winsvc.h, page updated 2025-07-01), verbatim:
     "When the service control manager starts a service process, it waits for the
     process to call the StartServiceCtrlDispatcher function. The main thread of a
     service process should make this call as soon as possible after it starts up
     (within 30 seconds)." And the error the SCM returns when nobody does:
     ERROR_FAILED_SERVICE_CONTROLLER_CONNECT — "This error is returned if the
     program is being run as a console application rather than as a service."
     ⇒ A bare node.exe performs no handshake, so it can NEVER be a Windows
     Service. Wrapping it in a third-party service host (nssm, winsw…) is the
     usual answer and it is REFUSED here: it adds an unversioned supervisor to a
     framework whose whole point is that the OS supervises. Task Scheduler is the
     NATIVE path and it is the one taken.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">

  <!--
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    🔴 NO SOCKET ACTIVATION ON WINDOWS — AND IT IS SETTLED TWICE OVER
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    On Linux a socket unit lets the OS hold the listening socket, so the daemon
    can stand down on a code change without any connection being refused. Nothing
    here can do that:
    ① Task Scheduler has no notion of a socket. Its action contract is a command
       line (see the Exec element below) — there is no element that would create,
       hold or hand over a descriptor.
    ② Node closes the question even if something did. Official net documentation
       (Node v22): "Listening on a file descriptor is not supported on Windows."
       So there is no API on this platform to listen on an inherited handle.
    ⚠️ The nearest Windows mechanism is WAS / net.tcp activation, and it does NOT
       apply: it activates MANAGED WCF applications hosted under IIS, never a bare
       node.exe. Bending it into shape would mean the third-party supervisor this
       file already refuses further down.
    ⇒ Windows keeps the eager model: logon trigger + restart on the completion
       event. The gap is DECLARED, never simulated — the daemon does have a
       silent window here between an exit 90 and its next start.
  -->

  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>ctxroute HTTP injection gate (local loopback, user scope)</Description>
    <URI>\ctxroute-http</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>

  <Triggers>

    <!--
      ⚠️ START AT LOGON, for THIS user only. Edit UserId (DOMAIN\user or
         MACHINE\user — `whoami` prints the exact string). Leaving it out makes
         the task fire for every user who logs on, and each of them would read
         somebody else's documents.
    -->
    <LogonTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <UserId>CHANGE_ME\CHANGE_ME</UserId>
    </LogonTrigger>

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🔴 THE RESTART-ON-EXIT-CODE TRIGGER — READ THIS BEFORE DELETING IT
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🛑 RestartOnFailure (below) DOES NOT SEE THE EXIT CODE. Its Learn page says
         only "if the task fails for any reason", which reads like it would; the
         normative text does not. [MS-TSCH] section RestartOnFailure, page updated
         2024-04-23, verbatim: "If the task fails to run because one of the start
         conditions is not met […] or because of a failure to start an action, the
         operation is attempted again."
         ⇒ It covers FAILING TO START. An action that started, ran, and exited 90
         is a task that COMPLETED. Windows would leave the daemon dead.
      ✅ What Windows does expose is the completion EVENT: Task Scheduler writes
         event 201 ("action completed") to its Operational channel, carrying the
         action's ResultCode. So the OS still TELLS us — we subscribe, we do not
         poll, we do not probe, we infer nothing. This is the same doctrine as the
         `fs.watch` in http-server.js: the kernel already knows, ask it once.
      ⚠️ THE CHANNEL MUST BE ENABLED OR THIS TRIGGER IS INERT — silently. Enabling
         it is also where the log ceiling gets declared, one gesture, see README.
      🔴 UNVERIFIED WITHOUT INSTALLING, and stated as such: that this exact XPath
         (an EventData filter on ResultCode and TaskName) matches on a live
         machine has NOT been measured here. Verify it with `wevtutil qe` against
         a real 201 event BEFORE relying on it, and fix this one line if the
         data names differ.
    -->
    <EventTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <Subscription>&lt;QueryList&gt;&lt;Query Id="0" Path="Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational"&gt;&lt;Select Path="Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational"&gt;*[System[Provider[@Name='Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler'] and (EventID=201)]] and *[EventData[Data[@Name='TaskName']='\ctxroute-http']] and *[EventData[Data[@Name='ResultCode']!='0']]&lt;/Select&gt;&lt;/Query&gt;&lt;/QueryList&gt;</Subscription>
    </EventTrigger>

  </Triggers>

  <Principals>
    <!--
      ⚠️ InteractiveToken + LeastPrivilege: the task runs AS the logged-in user,
         unelevated. It reads that user's documents and binds a loopback port —
         it needs nothing more, and a service that asks for more than it needs is
         the one nobody dares install.
    -->
    <Principal id="Author">
      <UserId>CHANGE_ME\CHANGE_ME</UserId>
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>

  <Settings>

    <!--
      🛑 THE THREE DEFAULTS BELOW WOULD KILL THIS DAEMON SILENTLY. They are not
         style, they are corrections of the schema's own defaults (settingsType,
         page updated 2020-12-11), each verified individually.
    -->

    <!--
      ① ExecutionTimeLimit. Omitted, a task is capped (the console default is
         PT72H) and Windows STOPS it after three days — for a daemon that is an
         outage on a timer, three days after every reboot, with nothing in sight
         to explain it. Verbatim from the element's page: "A value of PT0S will
         enable the task to run indefinitely."
      🛑 NEVER remove this line and NEVER give it a duration.
    -->
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>

    <!--
      ② and ③ Battery. Both default to TRUE in the schema, which on any laptop
         means: the gate never starts unplugged, and it DIES the moment the cable
         is pulled. The daemon costs nothing when idle and its absence costs the
         agent its knowledge.
    -->
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>

    <!--
      ⚠️ IgnoreNew is already the schema default; it is written DOWN because this
         file depends on it. Two triggers can fire close together (a logon that
         follows a failure event) and a second instance must simply not start.
      🛑 This is NOT how duplicates are prevented — the KERNEL is, with
         EADDRINUSE on the port. This line only avoids asking it twice. Never
         replace either one with a "is it already running?" check.
    -->
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>

    <!--
      ⚠️ RestartOnFailure covers exactly what [MS-TSCH] says it covers: the task
         failing to START (node.exe missing, path wrong, profile not ready at
         logon). That is a real case and worth retrying — it is simply NOT the
         exit-code case, which the EventTrigger above handles.
      ⚠️ Interval is a DECLARED delay with a DECLARED motive: the schema's own
         minimum is PT1M and nothing shorter can be expressed. Nothing is inferred
         from it; it is a retry spacing, never a liveness verdict.
    -->
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>3</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>

    <!--
      ⚠️ CLEAN STOP. AllowHardTerminate lets the Task Scheduler service terminate
         the process on `schtasks /End`, on logoff and on shutdown. There is
         nothing to flush, so being terminated IS the clean stop here.
    -->
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>

    <!--
      ⚠️ Idle settings OFF. StopOnIdleEnd defaults to stopping the task when the
         machine stops being idle — for a background daemon that is another
         silent death. RunOnlyIfIdle stays false for the mirror reason.
    -->
    <IdleSettings>
      <StopOnIdleEnd>false</StopOnIdleEnd>
      <RestartOnIdle>false</RestartOnIdle>
    </IdleSettings>
    <RunOnlyIfIdle>false</RunOnlyIfIdle>

    <!--
      ⚠️ No network condition: the socket is 127.0.0.1. A daemon that waited for
         "a network" would refuse to start on a machine with none, while the
         loopback interface has always been there.
    -->
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>

    <!--
      ⚠️ Priority 5 (normal) instead of the schema default 7. The whole reason
         this daemon exists is latency: every agent action waits on its answer.
         Leaving it in the background band would spend the win it was built for.
    -->
    <Priority>5</Priority>

    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <StartWhenAvailable>false</StartWhenAvailable>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
  </Settings>

  <Actions Context="Author">
    <!--
      ⚠️ ABSOLUTE PATHS, MANDATORY. Edit both. `where node` prints the first;
         Command must be the .exe itself.
      🛑 NEVER `cmd.exe /c` here. It is forbidden across this repository's layers
         and it would also hand the SCM-less process to a shell that then owns the
         exit code we depend on.

      ⚠️ THE PORT — AND THE ONE ASYMMETRY BETWEEN THE THREE OSES, DECLARED.
         Task Scheduler XML has NO element to set an environment variable for an
         action; systemd has Environment= and launchd has EnvironmentVariables,
         Windows has neither. The action therefore INHERITS the user's
         environment. Default case: nothing to do — 8787 is the default compiled
         into http-server.js. To move it, set a USER environment variable
         (`setx CTXROUTE_HTTP_PORT 9001`) and log out and back in, see README.
      🔴 NOT MEASURED HERE: whether a task started at logon picks up a
         `setx` performed during the previous session. Verify before relying on a
         non-default port.
    -->
    <Exec>
      <Command>C:\Program Files\nodejs\node.exe</Command>
      <Arguments>C:\CHANGE_ME\ctxroute\src\hooks\http-server.js</Arguments>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>

</Task>
